# The Count's Portraitist (side quest, docs/story/side_quests.md): Tudor, who paints the Baron's wicker sun, was fetched
# to the castle by a carriage with no driver and kept eleven nights painting a woman from the count's memory. He came
# home with the count's paints and can't stop: every face he paints is hers, and he has sold three. **Twist:** what
# he paints from those paints is an eye of the castle, like the portraits on its walls, and the great canvas the count
# gave him to copy from (data/monsters/the_counts_likeness.json) is the eye they all see through. Burn it and the rest
# go blank. Reached from Tudor at his door on the east row in the evening (npc vallaki_sun_painter). Every line here
# is unvoiced for now.

# --- Tudor at his door, evenings (npc vallaki_sun_painter, the east row) ---

~ tudor
if quest.the_counts_portraitist == done
Tudor: I painted the Baron's dog today. It came out looking like a dog. I wept, a little. Don't tell the Baron.
-> END
endif
if flag.likeness_burned
-> tudor_after
endif
if flag.tudor_told
Tudor: The door's open. I don't go in after dark any more. They watch you.
-> END
endif
Narrator: A thin man in a painter's smock sits on his own doorstep in the last of the light, with his hands in his lap. They're stained, but not yellow. A red-gold that doesn't come off.
if guest:ireena
Narrator: He sees Ireena, and goes white, and stands up so fast his stool falls over.
Tudor: It's you. It's you. I've painted you forty times. I never saw you in my life.
endif
* Your hands. That isn't sun paint. -> tudor_hands
* Goodbye. -> END

~ tudor_hands
set tudor_told
if not quest.the_counts_portraitist
quest the_counts_portraitist asked
endif
Tudor: Last month a carriage came for me. Black horses, and nobody holding the reins. He wanted a portrait. Not of himself. He has no face to sit for, he said. He wanted me to paint someone from his memory.
Tudor: Eleven nights. He talked and I painted. A woman in white. He gave me his own paints for it; he said mine weren't good enough for her. When it was done he said keep it. Copy it. Sell the copies.
Tudor: I came home and I can't stop. Every face I paint is hers. I've sold three. The Baron has one in his hall. Lady Wachter bought one. And Urwin at the inn, for over the bar.
* [Arcana DC 14] (Look at the paint on his hands.) -> tudor_arcana | tudor_no_arcana
* Show us the studio. -> tudor_studio

~ tudor_arcana
set likeness_known
Narrator: Under the red-gold there's a faint grain, like the grain of an eye. The castle's portraits watch its halls for their master. These were painted with the same paint. Wherever one hangs in Vallaki, the castle can see the room.
interject tag:blunt: He's put the count's eyes in the Baron's hall. And over the Martikovs' bar.
-> tudor_studio

~ tudor_no_arcana
Narrator: Paint, very good paint, in a colour you've never seen. That's all you can tell.
-> tudor_studio

~ tudor_studio
set tudor_studio_open
Tudor: Go in, if you're going. Upstairs and to the back. The big one's against the far wall, the one he gave me to copy from. Burn the copies if you like; I've tried. They don't burn. Only the big one's ever looked as if it minded.
-> END

# --- The studio: the great canvas (prop likeness_canvas) ---

~ canvas
if flag.likeness_burned
Narrator: A blackened frame on the wall and nothing in it. The copies round the room are blank canvas now, every one, as if they'd never been painted.
-> END
endif
cutscene sq_counts_likeness
Narrator: Against the far wall, in a gilt frame taller than a door, a young woman in white looks out of the canvas with her hands folded. She is painted as no living painter could paint her. Her eyes are dark, and wet, and they move.
if guest:ireena
Narrator: She has Ireena's face exactly. Ireena takes a step back, and the painted woman's eyes follow her, and soften.
endif
if flag.likeness_known
Narrator: You know what it is now. Not a picture of a woman. A window, and someone on the other side of it, looking out.
endif
Narrator: Then the mouth moves, and the voice that comes out of it isn't a woman's.
the_counts_likeness: Tell Tudor his work pleases me. I like to see Vallaki. I like to see who comes and goes from the inn.
* (Put a light to the canvas.) -> burn
* Who are you talking to, out of her? -> voice

~ voice
the_counts_likeness: You know who. You hear me in the dark with your eyes shut. I see you with mine open. Isn't she lovely? I had to describe her for eleven nights. I'd forgotten nothing.
-> burn

~ burn
Narrator: Every portrait in the studio turns its painted head toward you at once, with a sound like pages turning. The great canvas leans out of its frame.
combat counts_likeness

# --- Tudor, once the canvas has burned ---

~ tudor_after
quest the_counts_portraitist done
Narrator: Tudor is sitting on his step with a blank canvas across his knees, turning it to the light as if he can't believe there's nothing on it.
Tudor: They went blank. All of them. The Baron's man came running to ask why the lady in the hall had gone and left only canvas. I told him it was a very bad varnish.
Tudor: Take these. His paints. I've scraped them out of the pots into clean ones and said a prayer over them, which I've never done for paint. They're only paint now. Very, very good paint. Paint what you need with it.
give marvelous_pigments
gold +150
approve godrick_pendlebrook +2 liriel_dawnsong +2 thistle +1 wren_featherfoot +2 ratatoille +2: You burned the count's eye out of Tudor's studio
-> END
